# .mutation-allowlist - surviving (LIVED) mutants accepted as already known.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR, precisely: the CI gate compares the mutants that survive IN A DIFF
# against this list, and fails on any that are not here. So it is a list of what has been
# examined and accepted -- not a list of what has been fixed.
#
# It is PRUNED, not appended to. An entry is removed when the tests start killing that mutant,
# because a stale entry is worse than a missing one: it says "we looked at this and accepted
# it" about something nobody looked at twice. Regenerate it with:
#
#   make mutate && python3 - <<'EOF'
#   import json
#   r = json.load(open("report.json"))
#   lived = {"%s %s:%d" % (m["type"], f["file_name"], m["line"])
#            for f in r["files"] for m in f["mutations"] if m["status"] == "LIVED"}
#   keep = [l.strip() for l in open(".mutation-allowlist")
#           if l.strip() and not l.startswith("#") and l.strip() in lived]
#   print("\n".join(keep))
#   EOF
#
# DELETING THIS FILE DOES NOT START FRESH. It switches the gate off:
# mutation.yml sees no allowlist, reports "gate NOT calibrated ... cannot block" and exits 0.
# Every surviving mutant then goes unnoticed, and the workflow does not run on pushes to main
# either, so nothing would notice that either.
#
# Format:  <MUTATOR> <file>:<line>
#
# Add an entry ONLY when the mutant is provably equivalent (no test can ever kill
# it, because the mutation does not change observable behaviour) or the gap is a
# conscious, accepted risk. Adding a real test gap here hides a real risk, and
# mutation testing exists precisely to surface those.

